.TH HD-INSTALLER 8 "October 2026" "hd-installer" "HyprDebian"
.SH NAME
hd-installer \- install HyprDebian
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hd-installer
.RB [ \-\-reset ]
.RB [ \-\-answers
.IR file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hd-installer
installs HyprDebian on the disks of this computer, from a live system.
It asks all its questions first, then runs the installation without
questions.
It installs the base system, the same on every computer; for a desktop,
install
.B hd-desktop
on it afterwards.
.PP
It installs the hd-* packages from the local repository of the HyprDebian
live cd,
.IR /srv/hyprdebian/repo ,
and the Debian packages from the Debian mirrors, so it needs internet access
but no HyprDebian repository.
The installed system keeps only the Debian repositories.
To get updates of the hd-* packages, link it to a HyprDebian repository
after the first boot with
.BR hd-link-repo (8):
.PP
.RS
.B hd-link-repo
.I url
.B \-\-suite
.I suite
.RE
.PP
The installation is a tree of tasks in phases.
A task runs once: when it succeeds it is marked done, and a rerun skips it.
After a failure, fix the cause and run
.B hd-installer
again; it continues where it stopped, and offers the answers given before.
The answers and the finished tasks are kept in
.IR /tmp/hd-installer ,
so a reboot of the live system starts over.
.PP
Run it as root.
.SH QUESTIONS
.TP
.B Suite
The Debian suite to install:
.B unstable
or
.BR trixie ,
the suites of HyprDebian;
.B unstable
by default.
.B hd-desktop
needs packages that are only in
.BR unstable .
.TP
.B System mirror
Whether the system, the ZFS pool and the swap, is mirrored on two disks;
not by default.
.TP
.B Encryption
Whether the ZFS pool is encrypted, with the password of the user as its
passphrase; by default.
.TP
.B System disks
The disk for the system, and with the mirror the second one.
.TP
.B Swap
The size of the swap partition on the disks of the system, in GiB; 2 by
default, 0 for no swap.
With the system mirrored, the swap is mirrored too.
.TP
.B ESP mirror
Whether there is an EFI system partition on two disks; by default when the
system is mirrored.
.TP
.B ESP disks
The disk for the EFI system partition, and with the mirror the second one;
by default the disks of the system.
.TP
.B User name
The user account; a lower case letter, then lower case letters, digits,
.B \-
and
.BR _ .
.TP
.B Password
The password of the user, and with encryption the passphrase of the ZFS
pool; at least 8 characters, typed twice.
It is never saved, so it is asked again on every run.
.TP
.B Full name
The full name of the user; it cannot contain
.B :
or
.BR , .
.TP
.B Host name
By default
.BR hyprdebian ;
lower case letters, digits and
.BR \- .
.TP
.B FQDN
The fully qualified domain name: the host name followed by its domain.
.TP
.B Wired interface
The wired interface, which gets its address with DHCP; by default the
first one.
Without a wired interface, there is none, without the question.
.TP
.B Wifi
Whether wifi is used, not by default, and then the wifi
interface, which also gets its address with DHCP.
Without a wifi interface, there is no wifi, without the question.
.PP
Interfaces are picked by number, by their path name, such as
.BR enp3s0 ,
which is the name they get on the installed system.
Each is shown with its name on the live system and its IPv4 addresses.
.TP
.B Wipe
Every disk picked above is wiped.
Each one must be confirmed by typing
.BR y ;
the confirmation is not saved.
This is the last question, so nothing is wiped before every question is
answered.
.PP
The EFI system partitions are asked apart from the system, so they can go
on other disks than the ZFS pool, such as a hardware RAID volume next to a
ZFS mirror on disks that the RAID controller passes through.
.PP
Disks are picked by number from a list of the whole disks, by their name in
.IR /dev/disk/by-id ,
which stays the same when the kernel names the disks in another order.
Each is shown with its kernel name, size and model.
A disk that is already picked for the other side of a mirror stays in the
list, so the numbers do not change, but cannot be picked again.
.SH ANSWERS FILE
With
.BR \-\-answers ,
the answers come from a JSON file, for an installation without questions,
such as that of a virtual machine.
The host name is the name of the file without its extension, such as
.B test
for
.IR test.json .
Every other answer is a key of the JSON object; a question that the file
does not answer is asked as usual.
An answer of the file is checked as a typed one would be, and an answer that
is not valid stops the installation.
An unknown key, or a key given twice, is an error.
.TP
.B suite
The suite, a string.
.TP
.B encrypt
.B true
or
.BR false .
.TP
.B system_disks
A list of one disk, or two for a mirror.
A disk may be named by any path, such as
.IR /dev/vdb ;
the installer stores its name in
.IR /dev/disk/by-id .
.TP
.B swap
The swap size in GiB, a whole number.
.TP
.B esp_disks
A list of one disk, or two for a mirror, for the EFI system partitions.
.TP
.BR user ", " password ", " fullname
Strings.
The file holds the password in plain text; keep it readable only by root.
Leave it out to have it asked.
.TP
.B domain
The domain; the FQDN is the host name, a dot and the domain.
.TP
.B iface
The wired interface, by its path name, such as
.BR enp1s0 .
.TP
.B wifi
.B false
for no wifi, or the wifi interface by its path name.
.TP
.B wipe
.B true
confirms the wipe of every disk picked;
.B false
aborts.
.TP
.B debconf
A list of lines for
.BR debconf\-set\-selections (1),
each
.IR "owner question type value" ,
such as
.BR "tzdata tzdata/Areas select Europe" .
They are set in the new system before its packages are installed, so apt
does not ask them, and the locales, the time zone, the keyboard and the
console font are configured without questions for the packages that have
selections:
.BR locales ,
.BR tzdata ,
.B keyboard\-configuration
and
.BR console\-setup .
The debconf questions without a selection are asked as usual.
.PP
An example:
.PP
.RS
.nf
{
    "suite": "unstable",
    "encrypt": false,
    "system_disks": ["/dev/vda"],
    "swap": 2,
    "esp_disks": ["/dev/vda"],
    "user": "rsq",
    "password": "changeme",
    "fullname": "Erik Van Hamme",
    "domain": "example.org",
    "iface": "enp1s0",
    "wifi": false,
    "wipe": true,
    "debconf": [
        "locales locales/locales_to_be_generated multiselect en_US.UTF\-8 UTF\-8",
        "locales locales/default_environment_locale select en_US.UTF\-8",
        "tzdata tzdata/Areas select Europe",
        "tzdata tzdata/Zones/Europe select Brussels",
        "keyboard\-configuration keyboard\-configuration/xkb\-keymap select be",
        "console\-setup console\-setup/charmap47 select UTF\-8"
    ]
}
.fi
.RE
.PP
The path of the file is kept with the answers, so a rerun without
.B \-\-answers
reads the same file again, as the password is never saved.
.SH PHASES
.TP
.B questions
Asks the questions above.
.TP
.B disk
Turns off the swap, stops the md arrays, unmounts everything below
.I /mnt
and exports the ZFS pools of the live system, such as those of an earlier
attempt, then wipes every disk
picked and discards it when the disk supports that.
.TP
.B partition
Partitions the disks.
The numbers of the partitions are the same on every disk: 1 is the EFI
system partition (1 GiB) on the ESP disks, 2 the swap and 3 the ZFS pool on
the system disks.
A disk that is only an ESP disk or only a system disk has only its own
partitions.
.TP
.B filesystem
Makes
.I /mnt
a private mount, then a FAT32 file system on every EFI system partition; the swap, an md
raid1 array on mirrored disks, which is only formatted and turned on without
encryption, as the encrypted swap gets a new key on every boot; and the ZFS
pool
.BR rpool ,
a mirror on mirrored disks, encrypted with the password when asked for.
It makes the datasets
.BR rpool/hyprdebian ,
the root of the new system, and
.BR rpool/home ,
and mounts them on
.IR /mnt .
There is no boot pool:
.I /boot
is a folder of the root, as the bootloader reads the kernels from the EFI
system partitions.
.TP
.B base
Installs the minimal base of Debian, the
.B minbase
variant of
.BR debootstrap (8)
with
.BR systemd ,
on the root, and writes the files that depend on the disks and the answers:
.IR /etc/hostname ,
.IR /etc/hosts ,
.IR /etc/fstab ,
.I /etc/crypttab
for the encrypted swap,
.I /etc/mdadm/mdadm.conf
for the mirrored swap, and the automount units of the EFI system partitions
on
.I /boot/efi
and
.IR /boot/efi2 .
It binds the local repository of the live cd into
.I /run
of the new system and adds it as a trusted apt source, has apt install no
recommends and download no translations while it runs, as hd-base does for
the installed system, adds the Debian repositories, sets the debconf
selections of the answers file, asks the
locales, the time zone and the keyboard, and installs the kernel
and ZFS.
It marks the other packages for the packages phase:
.BR hd-base ,
.BR hd-bootloader-txt ,
and for wifi
.BR iwd .
.TP
.B packages
Installs the marked packages.
Then it installs the firmware that the hardware needs with
.BR hd-firmware (8),
turns on the firewall,
.BR ufw (8),
which lets in ssh, copies
.I /etc/skel
into
.IR /root ,
configures the network with
.BR hd-network-iface (8)
and
.BR hd-network-render (8),
writes the bootloader to the EFI system partitions, signed with the Secure
Boot keys of
.BR hd-base ,
see
.BR hd-secboot (7),
and adds the firmware boot entries.
.TP
.B user
Makes the user account, with the full name and the password, and its home
directory on a dataset of its own,
.BR rpool/home/ \fIuser\fR,
filled from
.IR /etc/skel .
The user becomes a member of the groups
.BR adm ,
.BR audio ,
.BR cdrom ,
.BR dip ,
.BR input ,
.BR plugdev ,
.B sudo
and
.BR video .
.TP
.B cleanup
Removes the apt source of the local repository of the live cd and the apt
config of the installer, empties the cache of apt, takes the
snapshot
.B rpool/hyprdebian@install
of the installed system, unmounts it and exports the pool, so the new
system imports it when it boots.
Then it shows how to link the new system to a HyprDebian repository.
The filesystem phase makes
.I /mnt
a private mount before the pool is mounted, so no service of the live system
keeps a copy of the mounts, which would keep the pool busy.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-reset
Forget the answers and the finished tasks, and start over.
.TP
.BI \-\-answers " file"
Take the answers from the JSON file, named
.IR "host name" .json,
see
.BR "ANSWERS FILE" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show a short help and exit.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 when the installation is done, 1 when a task failed, 2 on wrong
arguments.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /tmp/hd-installer/config
The answers, the path of the answers file, and the tasks that the answers
added.
.TP
.I /tmp/hd-installer/*.done
The finished tasks.
.TP
.I /usr/lib/hd-installer
The installer and its phases.
.TP
.I /srv/hyprdebian/repo
The local repository of the live cd, which the hd-* packages are installed
from.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR hd-base (7),
.BR hd-bootloader (7),
.BR hd-link-repo (8),
.BR hd-network (7),
.BR hd-firmware (8),
.BR hd-secboot (7)
